A||||2||Archie||A Good article on TPI wiring||||1||
Z||000000||Archie||11-02-2000||09:02 AM||||The December 2000 issue of Kit Car Illustrated has a very good article on TPI installation and wiring. The article deals with doing TPI on a hot rod, but 95% f the information would apply to the Fiero V-8.<P>Archie ||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||11-02-2000||09:20 AM||||Archie,<P>I bought the book from JTR on T.P.I. wiring. They go to great lengths to tell you how good the factory harness is. But, they also tell you to buy the factory manual from the donor car before you attempt to use the factory harness. I noticed you prefer to use an aftermarket harness (Painless?). Have you tried using the factory harness and had poor results? I've got a T.P.I. 350 that will (one day) go into either a Fiero or a 65 SS Nova and would like to hear from someone whos "been there, done that".<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy||||reg||5
Z||000002||Archie||11-02-2000||10:40 AM||||Roy, <P>I have that JTR book too. I've used original TPI wire harnesses from time to time on my own cars but. On customer cars, which are 80% of what we build here, I don't like to use used or modified parts like that. I feel that the customer pays for a premium V-8 swap, he should be getting the best new parts I can buy. So, as a matter of habit, 99% of the time, I'm thinking NEW specifically designed parts.<P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
